// What a generic module refuses once names are bound.
//
// A generic module's names belong to each of its instances, so the generic is
// named with type arguments wherever a member is wanted -- 'stack[i64].push'
// -- and nothing of it is folded or extended: not a path through its bare name,
// not a standalone 'use', not an import's 'use' clause, not an 'extends', not a
// default fold on its own 'mod' line. And an instance is cloned from the
// generic's functions, globals, plain types, overload names and type aliases:
// what it is not built for yet is refused where the generic declares it.
//
// Name resolution stage. The modules beside this file are its submodules:
//   stack.cone      a generic module holding a generic function and a trait
//   extender.cone   'extends' naming the generic module
//   folding.cone    an import of it with a 'use' clause
//   defaulted.cone  a generic module whose 'mod' line names a default fold
//   holder/         a generic module with a submodule of its own

use stack;                    //~ ErrorGenModBare:5

fn main() i32 {
  stack.push(1i64);           //~ ErrorGenModBare:3
  0i32;
}
